uses on testing innovative  ways to make the state more accountable  to its citizens  such as strengthening women's economic  and political opportunities ensuring  that environmental regulations reduce  harmful emissions  and providing citizens effective means  to voice their demands for state  services  so i'd love to open this conversation  with sunil  and sunil you really championed this  topic as one we should feature in the  yale development dialogue so  i wonder if you can kind of set the  scene for us  what is what does it look like and what  are some of the challenges in terms of  this perceived trade-off and can you  take us through some of the history of  environmental protection and development  being seen as a trade-off  thank you catherine and thanks everyone  for joining us um this was really  brought home to me just last month  when we had um the collapse or partial  collapse of two dams in uttar khan  in northern india um many people were  killed most of them workers on the site  and i was struck that for more than a  decade environmentalists and the courts  have been warning that that sort of  infrastructural development in such an  ecologically fragile region  is reckless that a catastrophe like this  was was almost inevitable  and yet these projects plowed on and one  of the first  responses to the disaster by the chief  minister of uttar khan  was to very quickly point out that we  shouldn't let what has happened  lead people to adopt an anti-development  narrative and that really gave me pause  because i realized in a sense how  pervasive that view is  the idea that environmental concern is  anti-development  and as a historian i'm very curious and  concerned with where  that pattern of thinking came from and  i'd like to maybe just give you  two or three examples because i think we  need to go back to the 1960s 1970s to  really understand it  um the first example is is rachel carson  and silent spring  carson is rightly celebrated i think as  a