ying that in practice of course  this turns out to be unbelievably  difficult right i mean so  i spent a lot of time crashing around  the city of london trying to work with  the insurance industry trying to work  with different forms of an  investment fund trying to define green  investment funds  and the amount of money in the end that  we actually got  available to be invested was  surprisingly limited  um because this seems to be  fundamentally  a problem um generally speaking  which is that these things are not as  profitable  as we want to pretend there is a reason  why generally speaking um effectively  public grant money  is more likely to be able to go into  some of these sectors than  private capital um and it's a lesson we  learn  in a hundred different ways i mean you  know one of the most depressing things  about development in africa  is that it's very easy to make a  presentation about the extraordinary  potential of africa the incredible human  capital the incredible natural capital a  great story about how this is the future  uh and you make those presentations  again and again while being aware that  british airways is shutting all its  flights into africa the major  hedge funds are drawing out of africa  barclays bank is getting rid of all its  branches in africa  um so there is a there's  there is a problem that we keep  returning to which is the theory of this  has to be right but nobody has yet  managed to crack this at scale  thank you rory any other thoughts on  those questions otherwise we have plenty  more coming in i'm thrilled with our  engagement here i want to i want to go  to this question um  from uh again sanjeev kumar here  um and i think it's very well put and i  wonder if this is something sunil might  speak to  sanjeev asks my sense is that the  philosophical foundation  undergirding the work of researchers and  policy makers  is too narrow to empathize with the pain  the current course of development and  growth  is inflicting on